      "author": {
        "id": 1006732
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-16T19:25:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Answer: the stored unit is Gbps, and the Arista `*1000` is correct for EOS.\n\nEvidence: your speed feature commit e20a5b0b documents a stored 25 programming `speed 25g`; docs/quads-api.md examples use `\"speed\": 10`; the web UI labels it Gbps (web/forms.py:32, web/templates/wiki/available.html:105, connectivity_cloud.html:640); `Interface.speed` is an Integer column. EOS takes Mbps, so `N * 1000` is the same value expressed in EOS units, and both drivers agree.\n\nWhat was wrong was the documentation: README.md and the new docs/switch-host-setup.md example both said `--interface-speed 1000`, which is nonsense under the Gbps convention and programs `speed 1000000`. PS9 states the unit in docs/switch-host-setup.md:160 and in the driver, adds `description: Link speed in Gbps` plus `example: 10` to `speed` in swagger.yaml, and corrects the README examples to 10.\n\nOne thing to confirm on the hardware: the EOS 4.36 command reference only lists letter forms for `speed` (`speed 10gfull` and friends), so I could not verify from documentation that a bare Mbps integer is accepted. The unit question is settled; the accepted form is not, and the now-live `%` detection will fail the port loudly if EOS rejects `speed \u003cN*1000\u003e`.\n\nNot added: boundary validation. The API already answers 400 for a negative or non-integer speed (interfaces.py:75,156 plus the app-wide ValueError handler), and an upper bound would reject values that exist in databases today with no escape hatch. Note a port stored as 1000 fails on Juniper at `commit` (`speed 1000g`), so that data needs correcting rather than a driver change.",
      "parentUuid": "f5d4ea80_f2ce77e5",
      "revId": "5ec35ddcb58a94477c496dce5f20d1f94223a05f",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
